<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-equations-of-normals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Equations of Normals</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.2 Differentiation I
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine the equations of tangents and normal to a curve
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To build physical intution for normals to a curve
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
        <p>
            Here is guidance for each part:
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use a physical demonstration if possible. Try to lead learners towards the idea that the angle made with the vertical (the normal) should be the same on both sides.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Try and get learners to apply the principle from (a), by reflecting over a normal. 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Connect the bounces to the normal. Outline how, if we zoom in on <m>y = x^2</m>, the curve is approximately flat (with slope <m>\frac{dy}{dx} = 2x</m>).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Learners should construct normals (the black lines) at all the contact points, building off the example. Their lines should all converge at <m>(0, 0.25)</m>, but do not worry if they don't.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Guide learners towards the idea that reflecting across the normal applies to all incoming balls, not just the vertical balls
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
            If time permits, show Learners <xref ref="parabola-focus"/> in the hint (this is also the answer to (d)). Ask them to speculate about why the lines all converge, and ask them to explain why this shape is useful for creating fire (answer: it concentrates energy from the sun).
        </p>
        <p>
            Very advanced students can be guided into proving that all lines meet at <m>(0, 0.25)</m>. You might need to help them by reminding them that <m>\frac{dy}{dx} = \tan \theta</m>, where <m>\theta</m> is the angle between a tangent and the positive horizontal, and provide the identity <m>\tan 2\theta = \frac{2\tan \theta}{1-\tan^2 \theta}</m>.
        </p>
    </axiom>

    <activity>
        <statement>
            <p> In <xref ref="wall-reflection"/>, a soccer ball is kicked into a flat wall. 
            <figure xml:id="wall-reflection">
                <caption>A ball hit into a horizontal wall</caption>
                <image>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[>=Stealth]
                            \draw[very thick, red!70!black] (-5,0) -- (5,0);
                            \draw[very thick, -&gt;, green!55!black] (-4.5,2.5) -- (0,0);
                            \draw[black, fill=gray!40] (-4.5,2.5) circle (0.3cm);
                            \draw[black, dashed] (0,0) -- (0,2.5);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p xml:id="flat-q">
                        What path do you think it will take after hitting the wall? Try drawing your suggestion, and compare with a partner. How might the dashed line help?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p xml:id="curved-single">
                        What if the ball is bounced into a curved wall, like the parabola <m>y = x^2</m> shown in <xref ref="parabola-reflection"/>? Draw the path you think it will take.
                        <figure xml:id="parabola-reflection">
                            <caption>A ball hit into a curved wall</caption>
                            <image>
                                <latex-image>
                                    \begin{tikzpicture}[
                                        x=1.4cm,y=1.4cm,
                                        >=Stealth,
                                        line cap=round,line join=round,
                                        axis/.style={->,thick},
                                        curve/.style={very thick,red!70!black},
                                        inRay/.style={->,thick,green!55!black},
                                        normal/.style={black},
                                        refRay/.style={->,thick,violet!80!black},
                                        hit/.style={fill=black,draw=none}
                                    ]
                                        % Window
                                        \def\xmin{-1.2}
                                        \def\xmax{ 1.2}
                                        \def\ymin{-0.2}
                                        \def\ymax{ 2.2}

                                        % Optional light grid
                                        \draw[step=0.25,gray!20] (\xmin,\ymin) grid (\xmax,\ymax);

                                        % Axes
                                        \draw[axis] (\xmin,0) -- (\xmax,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                        \draw[axis] (0,\ymin) -- (0,\ymax) node[above] {$y$};

                                        % ticks (every 0.5) and labels
                                        \foreach \x in {-1, 1}{
                                            \draw (\x,0.06) -- (\x,-0.06);              % x tick
                                            \node[below,scale=0.6, black] at (\x,-0.05) {$\x$};    % x label
                                        }
                                        \foreach \y in {1, 2}{
                                            \draw (0.06,\y) -- (-0.06,\y);              % y tick
                                            \node[left,scale=0.6, black] at (-0.05,\y) {$\y$};     % y label
                                        }

                                        % Parabola y = x^2
                                        \draw[curve,domain=\xmin:\xmax,samples=300] plot (\x,{(\x)^2}) ;

                                        % Vertical incoming rays every 0.5
                                        \foreach \a in {-1}{
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\ya}{\a*\a}
                                            \draw[inRay] (\a,\ymax) -- (\a,\ya);
                                            \fill[hit] (\a,\ya) circle (1.0pt);
                                        }

                                        % Normals + reflected rays for a != 0
                                        % Normal at (a,a^2): y-a^2 = -(1/(2a))(x-a)
                                        % Reflection slope: m_r = a - 1/(4a)
                                        \foreach \a in {-1}{
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\ya}{\a*\a}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\dxn}{(0.3)/sqrt(1/\ya + 4)}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\xL}{\a-\dxn}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\xR}{\a+\dxn}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\yL}{\ya + \dxn/(2*\a)}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\yR}{\ya - \dxn/(2*\a)}
                                            \draw[normal] (\xL,\yL) -- (\xR,\yR);
                                        }

                                    \end{tikzpicture}
                                </latex-image>
                            </image>
                        </figure>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p xml:id="curved-facts">
                        What facts about the curved wall are useful in helping you find the path? What might the dashed black line represent?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p xml:id="curved-many">
                        Try drawing the paths for all the balls in <xref ref="parabola-many-reflection"/>. What do you notice?
                        <figure xml:id="parabola-many-reflection">
                            <caption>Many balls hitting a curved wall</caption>
                            <image>
                                <latex-image>
                                    \begin{tikzpicture}[
                                        x=1.4cm,y=1.4cm,
                                        >=Stealth,
                                        line cap=round,line join=round,
                                        axis/.style={->,thick},
                                        curve/.style={very thick,red!70!black},
                                        inRay/.style={->,thick,green!55!black},
                                        normal/.style={black},
                                        refRay/.style={->,thick,violet!80!black},
                                        hit/.style={fill=black,draw=none}
                                    ]
                                        % Window
                                        \def\xmin{-1.2}
                                        \def\xmax{ 1.2}
                                        \def\ymin{-0.2}
                                        \def\ymax{ 2.2}

                                        % Optional light grid
                                        \draw[step=0.25,gray!20] (\xmin,\ymin) grid (\xmax,\ymax);

                                        % Axes
                                        \draw[axis] (\xmin,0) -- (\xmax,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                        \draw[axis] (0,\ymin) -- (0,\ymax) node[above] {$y$};

                                        % ticks (every 0.5) and labels
                                        \foreach \x in {-1, 1}{
                                            \draw (\x,0.06) -- (\x,-0.06);              % x tick
                                            \node[below,scale=0.6, black] at (\x,-0.05) {$\x$};    % x label
                                        }
                                        \foreach \y in {1, 2}{
                                            \draw (0.06,\y) -- (-0.06,\y);              % y tick
                                            \node[left,scale=0.6, black] at (-0.05,\y) {$\y$};     % y label
                                        }

                                        % Parabola y = x^2
                                        \draw[curve,domain=\xmin:\xmax,samples=300] plot (\x,{(\x)^2}) ;

                                        % Normals + reflected rays for a != 0
                                        % Normal at (a,a^2): y-a^2 = -(1/(2a))(x-a)
                                        % Reflection slope: m_r = a - 1/(4a)
                                        \foreach \a in {-1,-0.75, -0.5, -0.25, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1}{
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\ya}{\a*\a}
                                            \draw[inRay] (\a,\ymax) -- (\a,\ya);
                                            \fill[hit] (\a,\ya) circle (1.0pt);

                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\dxn}{(0.3)/sqrt(1/\ya + 4)}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\xL}{\a-\dxn}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\xR}{\a+\dxn}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\yL}{\ya + \dxn/(2*\a)}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\yR}{\ya - \dxn/(2*\a)}
                                            \draw[normal] (\xL,\yL) -- (\xR,\yR);

                                            % Reflected ray direction (forced upward)
                                            %\pgfmathsetmacro{\mr}{\a - 1/(4*\a)}
                                            %\pgfmathsetmacro{\ang}{atan(\mr)}
                                            %\pgfmathsetmacro{\angIn}{(\a &gt; 0) ? \ang+180 : \ang}
                                            %\pgfmathsetmacro{\len}{sqrt(\a*\a*\a*\a + 0.5*\a*\a + 1/16) + 0.2}
                                            %\draw[refRay] (\a,\ya) -- ++(\angIn:\len);

                                            \draw[black, fill=gray!40] (\a, \ymax) circle (0.08);
                                        }

                                    \end{tikzpicture}
                                </latex-image>
                            </image>
                        </figure>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Where would balls that didn't arrive vertically go? How would you find their reflection?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We've drawn the bounces of all these balls in <xref ref="parabola-focus"/>. Where do they all intersect? And why do they all intersect!
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="parabola-focus">
                <caption>The bounce paths of all the balls</caption>
                                            <image>
                                <latex-image>
                                    \begin{tikzpicture}[
                                        x=1.4cm,y=1.4cm,
                                        >=Stealth,
                                        line cap=round,line join=round,
                                        axis/.style={->,thick},
                                        curve/.style={very thick,red!70!black},
                                        inRay/.style={thick,green!55!black},
                                        normal/.style={black},
                                        refRay/.style={thick,violet!80!black},
                                        hit/.style={fill=black,draw=none}
                                    ]
                                        % Window
                                        \def\xmin{-1.2}
                                        \def\xmax{ 1.2}
                                        \def\ymin{-0.2}
                                        \def\ymax{ 2.2}

                                        % Optional light grid
                                        \draw[step=0.25,gray!20] (\xmin,\ymin) grid (\xmax,\ymax);

                                        % Axes
                                        \draw[axis] (\xmin,0) -- (\xmax,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                        \draw[axis] (0,\ymin) -- (0,\ymax) node[above] {$y$};

                                        % ticks (every 0.5) and labels
                                        \foreach \x in {-1, 1}{
                                            \draw (\x,0.06) -- (\x,-0.06);              % x tick
                                            \node[below,scale=0.6, black] at (\x,-0.05) {$\x$};    % x label
                                        }
                                        \foreach \y in {1, 2}{
                                            \draw (0.06,\y) -- (-0.06,\y);              % y tick
                                            \node[left,scale=0.6, black] at (-0.05,\y) {$\y$};     % y label
                                        }

                                        % Parabola y = x^2
                                        \draw[curve,domain=\xmin:\xmax,samples=300] plot (\x,{(\x)^2}) ;

                                        % Normals + reflected rays for a != 0
                                        % Normal at (a,a^2): y-a^2 = -(1/(2a))(x-a)
                                        % Reflection slope: m_r = a - 1/(4a)
                                        \foreach \a in {-1,-0.75, -0.5, -0.25, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1}{
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\ya}{\a*\a}
                                            \draw[inRay] (\a,\ymax) -- (\a,\ya);
                                            \fill[hit] (\a,\ya) circle (1.0pt);

                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\dxn}{(0.3)/sqrt(1/\ya + 4)}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\xL}{\a-\dxn}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\xR}{\a+\dxn}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\yL}{\ya + \dxn/(2*\a)}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\yR}{\ya - \dxn/(2*\a)}
                                            \draw[normal] (\xL,\yL) -- (\xR,\yR);

                                            % Reflected ray direction (forced upward)
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\mr}{\a - 1/(4*\a)}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\ang}{atan(\mr)}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\angIn}{(\a &gt; 0) ? \ang+180 : \ang}
                                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\len}{sqrt(\a*\a*\a*\a + 0.5*\a*\a + 1/16) + 0.2}
                                            \draw[refRay] (\a,\ya) -- ++(\angIn:\len);

                                            \draw[black, fill=gray!40] (\a, \ymax) circle (0.08);
                                        }

                                    \end{tikzpicture}
                                </latex-image>
                            </image>
            </figure>

            <note>
                <p> The point of intersection is called the <term>focus</term> of a parabola, and it is used in lenses and mirrors to create an image. You could even start a fire by placing metal in a parabolic shape around some kindling! </p>
            </note>
        </solution>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To connect the activity result to the algebraic definition and formula for
                    the normal line.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Open by asking: <em>"In the activity, what was the dashed line at each
                    contact point?"</em>
                    Let learners articulate that it was perpendicular to the surface — to the
                    tangent. Then ask: <em>"If the tangent has gradient <m>f'(a)</m>, what is
                    the gradient of a line perpendicular to it?"</em>
                    This leads naturally to the negative-reciprocal rule.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <p>
        In the activity, the dashed line at each contact point was perpendicular to the curve — that is, perpendicular to the tangent at that point.
        We call this line the <term>normal</term> to the curve.
        Since the tangent at <m>(a, f(a))</m> has gradient <m>f'(a)</m>,
        and two perpendicular lines satisfy <m>m_1 \cdot m_2 = -1</m>,
        the normal has gradient <m>-\dfrac{1}{f'(a)}</m>.
        Applying the point-slope form with this gradient gives the normal line formula.
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="insight-normal-formula">
        <title>Equation of a Normal Line</title>
        <p>
            The normal to the curve <m>y = f(x)</m> at the point <m>x = a</m> is:
            <md>y = -\frac{1}{f'(a)}(x - a) + f(a)</md>
            where <m>-\dfrac{1}{f'(a)}</m> is the gradient of the normal and
            <m>\bigl(a,\, f(a)\bigr)</m> is the point of contact.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <insight xml:id="insight-normal-procedure">
        <title>How to Find a Normal Line</title>
        <p>Given <m>f(x)</m>, to find the normal at <m>x = a</m>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><p>Find <m>f'(x)</m> and evaluate at <m>x = a</m> to get the tangent gradient <m>f'(a)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>The normal gradient is <m>-\dfrac{1}{f'(a)}</m>. Evaluate <m>f(a)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use point-slope form: <m>y = -\dfrac{1}{f'(a)}(x - a) + f(a)</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-normal-concept">
        <caption>
            The tangent (orange) and normal (teal) to <m>y = f(x)</m> at <m>(a, f(a))</m>. The normal is perpendicular to the tangent: their gradients multiply to <m>-1</m>. Drag <m>P</m> to see both lines rotate.
        </caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-normal-explorer" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/normal-explorer.js">
            <slate xml:id="normal-explorer" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>Drag <m>P</m> along the curve. The right-angle marker confirms the normal stays perpendicular to the tangent; their gradients multiply to <m>-1</m>. At the vertex the normal is vertical.</p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <warning>
        <p> If <m>f'(a) = 0</m> (horizontal tangent), the normal is the vertical line <m>x = a</m>. The previous formula doesn't work here since it would involve division by zero, but the normal is still well-defined as the line perpendicular to the tangent. </p>
    </warning>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-normal-quadratic">
        <title>Normal to a Quadratic</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the equation of the normal to <m>f(x) = x^2 - 4x</m> at <m>x = 3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1: Find the tangent gradient.</alert>
                By the power rule, <m>f'(x) = 2x - 4</m>.
                At <m>x = 3</m>:
                <md>f'(3) = 2(3) - 4 = 2</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2: Find the normal gradient and the point.</alert>
                Normal gradient <m>= -\dfrac{1}{f'(3)} = -\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
                <md>f(3) = 3^2 - 4(3) = 9 - 12 = -3</md>
                The point of contact is <m>(3, -3)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 3: Write the normal.</alert>
                <md>
                    <mrow>y \amp= -\frac{1}{2}(x - 3) + (-3)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= -\frac{x}{2} + \frac{3}{2} - 3</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= -\frac{x}{2} - \frac{3}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-normal-cubic">
        <title>Normal to a Cubic</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the equation of the normal to <m>f(x) = x^3</m> at <m>x = 2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1: Find the tangent gradient.</alert>
                <m>f'(x) = 3x^2</m>, so <m>f'(2) = 3(4) = 12</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2: Find the normal gradient and the point.</alert>
                Normal gradient <m>= -\dfrac{1}{12}</m>.
                <m>f(2) = 8</m>. Point of contact: <m>(2, 8)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 3: Write the normal.</alert>
                <md>
                    <mrow>y \amp= -\frac{1}{12}(x - 2) + 8</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= -\frac{x}{12} + \frac{1}{6} + 8</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= -\frac{x}{12} + \frac{49}{6}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-normal-gradient-condition">
        <title>Finding a Point from the Normal's Gradient</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all points on <m>f(x) = x^2 - 2x</m> where the normal is parallel to
                the line <m>y = x + 3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                A line parallel to <m>y = x + 3</m> has gradient <m>1</m>.
                We need the normal gradient to equal <m>1</m>, which means the tangent gradient
                must equal <m>-1</m> (since the product of perpendicular gradients is <m>-1</m>).
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>f'(x) = 2x - 2</m>:
                <md>2a - 2 = -1 \implies a = \frac{1}{2}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                At <m>x = \tfrac{1}{2}</m>:
                <m>f\!\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right) = \tfrac{1}{4} - 1 = -\tfrac{3}{4}</m>.
                Point of contact: <m>\left(\tfrac{1}{2},\, -\tfrac{3}{4}\right)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The normal with gradient <m>1</m> through this point:
                <md>
                    <mrow>y \amp= 1 \cdot \left(x - \frac{1}{2}\right) + \left(-\frac{3}{4}\right)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= x - \frac{5}{4}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-normals">

        <exercise xml:id="ex-normal-basic">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the equation of the normal to each curve at the given point.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = x^2 + 1</m> at <m>x = 2</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = x^3</m> at <m>x = 1</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = 2x^2 - 3x</m> at <m>x = 2</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = x^2 - 4x</m> at <m>x = 3</m></p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 2x</m>, <m>f'(2) = 4</m>.
                            Normal gradient <m>= -\tfrac{1}{4}</m>.
                            <m>f(2) = 5</m>. Point: <m>(2, 5)</m>.
                            <md>y = -\frac{1}{4}(x - 2) + 5 = -\frac{x}{4} + \frac{11}{2}</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 3x^2</m>, <m>f'(1) = 3</m>.
                            Normal gradient <m>= -\tfrac{1}{3}</m>.
                            <m>f(1) = 1</m>. Point: <m>(1, 1)</m>.
                            <md>y = -\frac{1}{3}(x - 1) + 1 = -\frac{x}{3} + \frac{4}{3}</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 4x - 3</m>, <m>f'(2) = 5</m>.
                            Normal gradient <m>= -\tfrac{1}{5}</m>.
                            <m>f(2) = 2</m>. Point: <m>(2, 2)</m>.
                            <md>y = -\frac{1}{5}(x - 2) + 2 = -\frac{x}{5} + \frac{12}{5}</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 2x - 4</m>, <m>f'(3) = 2</m>.
                            Normal gradient <m>= -\tfrac{1}{2}</m>.
                            <m>f(3) = -3</m>. Point: <m>(3, -3)</m>.
                            <md>y = -\frac{1}{2}(x - 3) + (-3) = -\frac{x}{2} - \frac{3}{2}</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                    <li><m>y = -\dfrac{x}{4} + \dfrac{11}{2}</m></li>
                    <li><m>y = -\dfrac{x}{3} + \dfrac{4}{3}</m></li>
                    <li><m>y = -\dfrac{x}{5} + \dfrac{12}{5}</m></li>
                    <li><m>y = -\dfrac{x}{2} - \dfrac{3}{2}</m></li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-normal-tangent-together">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x</m>.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the equation of the tangent to <m>f</m> at <m>x = 2</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the equation of the normal to <m>f</m> at <m>x = 2</m>.</p></li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the coordinates of the point where the normal crosses the
                            <m>x</m>-axis.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <p>
                    <m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3</m>, <m>f'(2) = 9</m>.
                    <m>f(2) = 2</m>. Point: <m>(2, 2)</m>.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Tangent: <m>y = 9(x - 2) + 2 = 9x - 16</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Normal gradient <m>= -\tfrac{1}{9}</m>.
                            <md>y = -\frac{1}{9}(x - 2) + 2 = -\frac{x}{9} + \frac{20}{9}</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Set <m>y = 0</m>: <m>\dfrac{x}{9} = \dfrac{20}{9} \implies x = 20</m>.
                            The normal crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at <m>(20,\, 0)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><m>y = 9x - 16</m></li>
                    <li><m>y = -\dfrac{x}{9} + \dfrac{20}{9}</m></li>
                    <li><m>(20,\, 0)</m></li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-normal-gradient-condition">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all points where the normal to the given curve has the specified gradient.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f(x) = x^2</m>, normal parallel to <m>y = -x + 5</m>
                            (i.e. normal gradient <m>= -1</m>).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f(x) = x^3</m>, normal gradient <m>= -\dfrac{1}{12}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Normal gradient <m>= -1</m> means tangent gradient <m>= 1</m>.
                            <md>f'(a) = 2a = 1 \implies a = \frac{1}{2}</md>
                            <m>f\!\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right) = \tfrac{1}{4}</m>.
                            Point: <m>\left(\tfrac{1}{2},\, \tfrac{1}{4}\right)</m>.
                            Normal:
                            <md>y = -\left(x - \tfrac{1}{2}\right) + \tfrac{1}{4} = -x + \frac{3}{4}</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Normal gradient <m>= -\tfrac{1}{12}</m> means tangent gradient <m>= 12</m>.
                            <md>f'(a) = 3a^2 = 12 \implies a^2 = 4 \implies a = \pm 2</md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            At <m>x = 2</m>: <m>f(2) = 8</m>. Normal:
                            <md>y = -\frac{1}{12}(x - 2) + 8 = -\frac{x}{12} + \frac{49}{6}</md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            At <m>x = -2</m>: <m>f(-2) = -8</m>. Normal:
                            <md>y = -\frac{1}{12}(x + 2) + (-8) = -\frac{x}{12} - \frac{49}{6}</md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            There are two such points: <m>(2, 8)</m> and <m>(-2, -8)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Point <m>\left(\tfrac{1}{2},\, \tfrac{1}{4}\right)</m>;
                        normal <m>y = -x + \dfrac{3}{4}</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Two points: <m>(2, 8)</m> and <m>(-2, -8)</m>, with normals
                        <m>y = -\dfrac{x}{12} + \dfrac{49}{6}</m>
                        and <m>y = -\dfrac{x}{12} - \dfrac{49}{6}</m> respectively.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-normal-meets-curve">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The normal to <m>f(x) = x^2</m> at a point <m>(a, a^2)</m>
                    (where <m>a \neq 0</m>) meets the curve again at a second point.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the normal to <m>y = x^2</m> at <m>x = 1</m>.
                            By solving simultaneously with <m>y = x^2</m>, show that the normal
                            meets the curve again at <m>x = -\dfrac{3}{2}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Show that in general the normal at <m>(a, a^2)</m> meets <m>y = x^2</m>
                            again at <m>x = -\dfrac{2a^2 + 1}{2a}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    For part (b), set <m>x^2 = -\dfrac{x}{2a} + a^2 + \dfrac{1}{2}</m>,
                    multiply through by <m>2a</m>, rearrange, and factor out <m>(x - a)</m>.
                </p>
            </hint>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Normal at <m>x = 1</m>: <m>f'(1) = 2</m>,
                            normal gradient <m>= -\tfrac{1}{2}</m>, <m>f(1) = 1</m>.
                            Normal: <m>y = -\tfrac{x}{2} + \tfrac{3}{2}</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Set equal to <m>x^2</m>:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>x^2 \amp= -\frac{x}{2} + \frac{3}{2}</mrow>
                                <mrow>2x^2 + x - 3 \amp= 0</mrow>
                                <mrow>(x - 1)(2x + 3) \amp= 0</mrow>
                            </md>
                            So <m>x = 1</m> (the contact point) or <m>x = -\tfrac{3}{2}</m>.
                            Check: <m>\left(-\tfrac{3}{2}\right)^2 = \tfrac{9}{4}</m> and
                            <m>-\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot\left(-\tfrac{3}{2}\right) + \tfrac{3}{2} = \tfrac{3}{4}+\tfrac{3}{2} = \tfrac{9}{4}</m>. ✓
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Normal at <m>(a, a^2)</m>: <m>y = -\dfrac{x}{2a} + a^2 + \dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Set equal to <m>x^2</m> and multiply by <m>2a</m>:
                            <md>2ax^2 + x - 2a^3 - a = 0</md>
                            Since <m>x = a</m> is a root, factor:
                            <md>(x - a)(2ax + 2a^2 + 1) = 0</md>
                            The other root: <m>x = -\dfrac{2a^2 + 1}{2a}</m>. ✓
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Normal <m>y = -\dfrac{x}{2} + \dfrac{3}{2}</m>;
                        second intersection at <m>\left(-\dfrac{3}{2},\, \dfrac{9}{4}\right)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li><p><m>x = -\dfrac{2a^2 + 1}{2a}</m>. See solution.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-normal-parallel">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider <m>f(x) = x^3</m>.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Show that the normals to <m>y = x^3</m> at <m>x = 1</m> and at
                            <m>x = -1</m> are parallel, and find the equation of each.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the <m>y</m>-intercept of each normal, and hence find the
                            perpendicular distance between them.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 3x^2</m>, so <m>f'(1) = 3</m> and <m>f'(-1) = 3</m>.
                            Both normals have gradient <m>-\tfrac{1}{3}</m> — parallel. ✓
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            At <m>x = 1</m>: <m>f(1) = 1</m>. Normal:
                            <md>y = -\frac{1}{3}(x - 1) + 1 = -\frac{x}{3} + \frac{4}{3}</md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            At <m>x = -1</m>: <m>f(-1) = -1</m>. Normal:
                            <md>y = -\frac{1}{3}(x + 1) + (-1) = -\frac{x}{3} - \frac{4}{3}</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>y</m>-intercepts: <m>\tfrac{4}{3}</m> and <m>-\tfrac{4}{3}</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Rewriting as <m>x + 3y - 4 = 0</m> and <m>x + 3y + 4 = 0</m>,
                            the distance between parallel lines <m>ax + by + c_1 = 0</m>
                            and <m>ax + by + c_2 = 0</m> is
                            <m>d = \dfrac{|c_1 - c_2|}{\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}}</m>:
                            <md>d = \frac{|-4 - 4|}{\sqrt{1 + 9}} = \frac{8}{\sqrt{10}} = \frac{4\sqrt{10}}{5}</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Both normals have gradient <m>-\tfrac{1}{3}</m>.
                        At <m>x=1</m>: <m>y = -\dfrac{x}{3} + \dfrac{4}{3}</m>.
                        At <m>x=-1</m>: <m>y = -\dfrac{x}{3} - \dfrac{4}{3}</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>y</m>-intercepts <m>\pm\dfrac{4}{3}</m>;
                        distance <m>\dfrac{4\sqrt{10}}{5}</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-normal-focus">
            <title>★ Proving the Focus of a Parabola</title>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    The activity showed that all vertical rays bouncing off the parabola
                    <m>y = x^2</m> converge to a single point. This exercise proves it using
                    the mathematics of normals and the law of reflection.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the equation of the normal to <m>y = x^2</m> at the general point
                        <m>(a, a^2)</m>. Show it has equation:
                        <md>y = -\frac{x}{2a} + a^2 + \frac{1}{2}</md>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Tangent gradient: <m>f'(a) = 2a</m>. Normal gradient: <m>-\dfrac{1}{2a}</m>.
                        <md>
                            <mrow>y - a^2 \amp= -\frac{1}{2a}(x - a)</mrow>
                            <mrow>y \amp= -\frac{x}{2a} + \frac{1}{2} + a^2</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>\varphi</m> be the angle the normal makes with the positive
                        <m>x</m>-axis, so <m>\tan\varphi = -\dfrac{1}{2a}</m>.
                        A vertical incoming ray arrives at <m>(a, a^2)</m> from above.
                        By the law of reflection (angle of incidence = angle of reflection,
                        both measured from the normal), the reflected ray makes angle
                        <m>2\varphi - 90°</m> with the horizontal.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Using the double-angle identity
                        <m>\tan 2\varphi = \dfrac{2\tan\varphi}{1 - \tan^2\varphi}</m>,
                        show that the gradient of the reflected ray is:
                        <md>m_r = \frac{4a^2 - 1}{4a}</md>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        With <m>\tan\varphi = -\dfrac{1}{2a}</m>:
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\tan 2\varphi \amp=
                                \frac{2 \cdot \left(-\frac{1}{2a}\right)}{1 - \frac{1}{4a^2}}
                                = \frac{-\frac{1}{a}}{\frac{4a^2 - 1}{4a^2}}
                                = \frac{-4a}{4a^2 - 1}</mrow>
                        </md>
                        Then:
                        <md>
                            <mrow>m_r \amp= \tan(2\varphi - 90°) = -\cot 2\varphi
                                = -\frac{1}{\tan 2\varphi}
                                = \frac{4a^2 - 1}{4a}</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write the equation of the reflected ray through <m>(a, a^2)</m>
                        with gradient <m>\dfrac{4a^2 - 1}{4a}</m>.
                        Show that this line passes through <m>\left(0,\, \dfrac{1}{4}\right)</m>
                        for every <m>a \neq 0</m>.
                        What does this prove about the parabola <m>y = x^2</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The reflected ray through <m>(a, a^2)</m>:
                        <md>y - a^2 = \frac{4a^2 - 1}{4a}(x - a)</md>
                        At <m>x = 0</m>:
                        <md>
                            <mrow>y \amp= a^2 + \frac{4a^2 - 1}{4a}(0 - a)
                                = a^2 - \frac{4a^2 - 1}{4}
                                = a^2 - a^2 + \frac{1}{4} = \frac{1}{4}</mrow>
                        </md>
                        The reflected ray passes through <m>\left(0, \tfrac{1}{4}\right)</m>
                        for every <m>a \neq 0</m>. ✓
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        This proves that the <term>focus</term> of the parabola <m>y = x^2</m>
                        is the point <m>\left(0,\, \dfrac{1}{4}\right)</m>:
                        all incoming vertical rays reflect through this single point.
                        In general, the focus of <m>y = \dfrac{x^2}{4p}</m> is <m>(0, p)</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Focus on activity parts (a)–(c) and Examples 1–2.
                        Provide a step card: (1) find <m>f'(a)</m> for tangent gradient,
                        (2) take negative reciprocal for normal gradient,
                        (3) find <m>f(a)</m>, (4) substitute into point-slope form.
                        Assign Exercise 1 only.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Full activity plus all three examples. Assign Exercises 1–4.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Assign all exercises including the focus proof (Exercise 6).
                        Extension: what is the focus of <m>y = 2x^2</m>?
                        (Write as <m>y = \dfrac{x^2}{4 \cdot (1/8)}</m>;
                        focus is <m>\left(0,\, \tfrac{1}{8}\right)</m>.)
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

